Title: GOLDEN HOUR
Credit: Written by
Author: A. Sample
Draft date: First draft

FADE IN:

EXT. VENICE BEACH BOARDWALK - LOS ANGELES - MAGIC HOUR

Skaters, a man playing "Hotel California" on a battered Fender, tourists with In-N-Out bags.

PRIYA NAIDU (29), a wedding photographer with a Canon R5 around her neck, jogs after a runaway ring bearer.

Her second shooter, TOMMY GALLAGHER (24), Vans and a Dodgers cap, films on a Sony FX3.

TOMMY
That's the fourth kid today.

PRIYA
Fifth. You missed the one who ate the boutonniere.

INT. THE SUNSET DIPLOMAT HOTEL - BALLROOM - NIGHT

Chandeliers. Three hundred guests. A banner reads MEHTA - WHITFIELD.

The bride, ANJALI MEHTA (31), a pediatric nurse at CEDARS-SINAI, hides in the kitchen with a slice of cake and a glass of Veuve Clicquot.

PRIYA
You're supposed to be doing the first dance.

ANJALI
The DJ is playing Ed Sheeran. I said no Ed Sheeran. I said it in writing.

INT. HOTEL KITCHEN - CONTINUOUS

The DJ, a nervous kid named MARCUS LEVINE, pokes his head in.

MARCUS
The groom's mother requested it.

ANJALI
The groom's mother requested a chocolate fountain and we said no to that too.

EXT. HOTEL PARKING STRUCTURE - NIGHT

Priya sits on the hood of her Subaru Outback, scrolling photos on an iPad. Tommy leans beside her with a Red Bull.

TOMMY
Bride's aunt asked if we do "AI touch-ups." She wants the uncle removed from every photo.

PRIYA
Which uncle?

TOMMY
The one who cried during the vows. She says it was "not a good cry."

PRIYA
We can do that. We charge two hundred an uncle.

Her phone rings. A number she doesn't recognize. 310-555-0177.

PRIYA (CONT'D)
(answering)
Golden Hour Photography.

A MAN'S VOICE, calm, expensive.

MAN (V.O.)
Ms. Naidu. My name is GREGORY ASHWORTH. I'm counsel for the Whitfield family. We'd like to discuss the photographs from tonight before they're delivered.

INT. ASHWORTH & KANE - CENTURY CITY - DAY

Glass box on Avenue of the Stars. GREGORY ASHWORTH (55), the kind of tan that costs money, slides a document across a walnut table.

ASHWORTH
The family would prefer that the photographs of Mr. Whitfield's brother -- the one who was, shall we say, emotional -- never exist.

PRIYA
They exist. I took them.

ASHWORTH
Then they'd like to purchase them. All of them. Exclusively.

He names a figure. Priya doesn't blink.

PRIYA
Why does a man crying at a wedding cost that much?

ASHWORTH
Because his name is Douglas Whitfield and there are two hundred people in Sacramento who think he was in Palm Springs that night.

EXT. GRIFFITH OBSERVATORY - DAY

Priya and Anjali on the lawn, city smeared with haze below.

ANJALI
Doug Whitfield is a state senator. I married into a family that lies about being at weddings.

PRIYA
So who was he supposed to be with in Palm Springs?

ANJALI
Nobody. That's the point. He was supposed to be at the Betty Ford Center.

INT. PRIYA'S STUDIO - CULVER CITY - NIGHT

A converted garage. Prints drying on a line. Priya backs up the wedding to a WD drive, then to a Backblaze account, then to a thumb drive she tapes under the desk.

Tommy watches, eating a Chipotle bowl.

TOMMY
You're not selling them.

PRIYA
I'm delivering them. To the client. Who is the bride. Who is my friend.

TOMMY
And the senator?

PRIYA
The senator can cry at his brother's wedding like everybody else.

FADE OUT.

THE END
